AIM:
To write a program to implement a Calculator using FLEX and BISON.
ALGORITHM:
Step 1: Start the program.
Step 2: In the definitions part of the FLEX file, include the regular definition for a digit.
Step 3: In the rules part of the FLEX file, specify the pattern for a number and its action (return NUM with the
numeric value in yylval) in cal.l.
Step 4: In the BISON program, define grammar rules so that all the arithmetic operations +, -, *, / are evaluated
using operator precedence.
Step 5: Display error if the input does not match the grammar.
Step 6: Provide the input.
Step 7: Verify the output.
Step 8: End.
PROCEDURE:
Create a file named cal.l and define patterns to identify numbers using regular expressions.
For matched digits, return the token NUM and store the number using yylval.
Create another file named cal.y to define grammar rules for arithmetic expressions using BISON.
Include operator precedence and associativity using %left and %right.
Add rules to evaluate expressions like E + E, E - E, E * E, and E / E.
Compile both files using the following commands:
o flex cal.l
o bison -d cal.y
o gcc lex.yy.c cal.tab.c -o calc -lfl
Run the compiled program: ./calc
Enter arithmetic expressions and view the result.
Test with multiple expressions to verify both valid and invalid cases.
PROGRAM:
cal.l
%{
#include &quot;cal.tab.h&quot;
%}
DIGIT [0-9]+
%option noyywrap
%%
{DIGIT} { yylval = atof(yytext); return NUM; }
\n|. { return yytext[0]; }
%%
cal.y
%{
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#define YYSTYPE double
%}
%token NUM
%left &#39;+&#39; &#39;-&#39;
%left &#39;*&#39; &#39;/&#39;
%right UMINUS
%%
Statment: E { printf(&quot;Answer: %g \n&quot;, $$); }
| Statment &#39;\n&#39;
;
E : E &#39;+&#39; E { $$ = $1 + $3; }
| E &#39;-&#39; E { $$ = $1 - $3; }
| E &#39;*&#39; E { $$ = $1 * $3; }

| E &#39;/&#39; E { $$ = $1 / $3; }
| NUM
;
%%
int main() {
printf(&quot;Enter the expression:\n&quot;);
yyparse();
return 0;
}
int yyerror(char *s) {
printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, s);
return 0;
}
OUTPUT:
$ flex cal.l
$ bison -d cal.y
$ gcc lex.yy.c cal.tab.c -o calc -lfl
$ ./calc
Enter the expression:
2+2
Answer: 4
RESULT:
Thus the program for implementing a calculator using FLEX and BISON was executed and verified successfully.
